<html xmlns:o="urn:schemas-microsoft-com:office:office" xmlns:w="urn:schemas-microsoft-com:office:word" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/TR/REC-html40"><head><meta charset="utf-8"><title>The feelings expressed in two loneliness interviews</title><!--[if gte mso 9]><xml><w:WordDocument><w:View>Print</w:View></w:WordDocument></xml><![endif]--><style>body,p,li,td{font-family:Cambria,Georgia,serif;font-size:11pt;line-height:1.35;color:#17130f}h1,h2,h3{font-family:Cambria,Georgia,serif;font-weight:normal;color:#8c1912}h1{font-size:20pt}h2{font-size:14pt}p.note{font-size:9pt}td,th{border:1px solid #e4dcd0;padding:3pt 5pt;font-size:9pt;vertical-align:top}table{border-collapse:collapse}</style></head><body><p style="color:#8b7f72;font-size:9pt">RUBICON REPORT</p><h1>The feelings expressed in two loneliness interviews</h1><p class="asked"><b>Question</b> In interviews I01 (person, 24, Newham) and I48 (woman, 23, Barking &amp; Dagenham), where does the interviewee express or report their own positive, negative or mixed feelings? For each example: its valence, a short label for the emotion, and what it is about; and how many examples of each valence come from each person. Base: the two people; no comparison beyond setting them side by side. Feelings the interviewer suggests and the interviewee does not take up, and feelings the interviewee attributes to other people, do not count.</p><p>The feelings the person from Newham (I01) expresses are negative in 6 passages, positive in 3 passages and mixed in 1 passage; those of the woman from Barking &amp; Dagenham (I48) are negative in 11 passages, positive in 7 passages and mixed in 1 passage. Both express more negative than positive feelings, and loneliness recurs among the negative ones of both. Each example is one feeling, or several named together in one answer, about one thing, counted once however often it is repeated. They place their loneliness in not being understood, after breakups and in the shopping centre where people sleep rough; she places hers at home, at night and in the loss of her mother. Their good feelings come from the barber shop&#x27;s familiar community, from time alone that they choose and from respect for people who help rough sleepers; hers come mostly from being with people, which keeps her thoughts at bay, and also from her love of children, her hope of a child of her own and her acceptance of her mother&#x27;s death.</p><h2 id="s-the-person-from-newham-i01">The person from Newham (I01)</h2>
<p>Their negative feelings centre on being misunderstood. They say &quot;I’ve always had people around me but I’ve been more misunderstood, which has led to being lonely&quot; (I01, “I’ve always had people around…”), and calls it &quot;quite frustrating&quot; (I01, “Uuum, quite frustrating. It’s frustrating…”). They also recall times &quot;where I felt unsupported&quot; (I01, “probably, a couple of times…”) and says that after their breakups &quot;you feel more lonelier after than you was before&quot; (I01, “I was always just as…”).</p>
<p>Walking through the shopping centre after 7pm past people sleeping rough brings out three feelings: loneliness (&quot;I think I feel quite lonely in that moment&quot; (I01, “I think I feel quite…”)), resentment towards those responsible (&quot;I feel completely resentment when I walk through those environments and see those people sleeping rough&quot; (I01, “I feel completely resentment when…”)), and doubt about whether to give money, which they call &quot;conflicting emotions&quot; (I01, “I wouldn’t want to contribute…”).</p>
<p>Their positive feelings are about the barber shop (&quot;it feels good to go, um I mean I enjoy the barber shop&quot;, and &quot;I’m grateful to see people that I know and have known for a long time fine&quot; (I01, “it feels good to go…”)), about time alone that they choose (&quot;I’m quite at ease with that situation&quot; (I01, “when I choose to kind…”)), and about people who run soup kitchens, of whom they say &quot;I respect and appreciate&quot; (I01, “I’ve seen how some people…”).</p>
<h2 id="s-the-woman-from-barking-dagenham-i48">The woman from Barking &amp; Dagenham (I48)</h2>
<p>Her negative feelings return again and again to being alone. Seeing friends with their mothers &quot;really makes me sad, it makes me feel lonely&quot; (I48, “it really makes me sad…”), and she says &quot;I wished I had my mom&quot; (I48, “Just, I wished I had…”); asked how she feels about not being able to sleep, she says &quot;I feel like I can’t change that. I can’t bring my mom back.&quot; (I48, “I feel like I can’t…”). Seeing friends with siblings is &quot;annoying, sad, lonely&quot; and &quot;makes me wish I can do what they do with their siblings&quot; (I48, “It’s annoying, sad, lonely. [pause]…”). Alone, she reports that &quot;It’s not a nice feeling, knowing that there’s no one&quot; (I48, “It’s not a nice feeling…”), that &quot;sometimes I just remember things and I’m not happy about that&quot; (I48, “sometimes I just remember things…”), &quot;I don’t feel okay&quot; (I48, “I don’t feel okay. I…”) and &quot;I feel like I need help sometimes&quot; (I48, “I feel like I need…”). At night she sometimes has &quot;to cry myself to sleep because I’m lonely&quot; (I48, “at night before going to…”); at home, when the house is quiet, she realises &quot;I’m lonely, starts all over again&quot; (I48, “then I realise, oh my…”) and feels bored (I48, “Just bored cause there’s nothing…”). About having no one to talk to about her day, she says &quot;I’m used to it now but sometimes it can get to me&quot; (I48, “I feel like I’m used…”).</p>
<p>Her positive feelings come mostly from company and activity: &quot;worry-free around my friends&quot; (I48, “It makes worry-free around my…”), &quot;I feel good, happy&quot; when around people (I48, “I feel good, happy”), fun when clubbing that &quot;makes me forget about, you know, life&quot; (I48, “I see people having fun…”), and &quot;Good, relieved&quot; about work and her colleagues there (I48, “Good, relieved”), where she also says &quot;I love kids so much&quot; (I48, “I love kids so much”). She also expresses hope of having her own child one day (I48, “I just hope that one…”), and says of her mother&#x27;s death &quot;It’s alright now. It’s cool.&quot; (I48, “It’s alright now. It’s cool”), an acceptance that sits beside her later longing for her mother (I48, “Just, I wished I had…”).</p>
<h2 id="s-side-by-side">Side by side</h2>
<p>The chart counts each person&#x27;s examples by whether the feeling is positive, negative or mixed.</p>

<p>They speak about loneliness at some distance, often in the second person and in reflective terms; she names her feelings briefly and directly, and several of her negative feelings are answers to the interviewer&#x27;s repeated questions about how something makes her feel. Most of the positive feelings of both come from being with familiar people, and for her they work mainly as relief from what she feels alone (see “The woman from Barking &amp; Dagenham (I48)”); against this, they also feel at ease in time alone that they choose (I01, “when I choose to kind…”).</p>
<h2 id="s-limits">Limits</h2>
<p>These are two interviews, so they describe two people and nothing wider. The counts depend on where one feeling ends and the next begins, and on excluding hypothetical feelings and feelings they imagine for figures in their drawings, which are a large part of their first interview; a different reader could split or merge some examples. Both interviews asked directly about loneliness, which draws out negative feelings.</p><h2>How this was made</h2><h3>What was read</h3><p>All 48 documents, read in full: 24 male, 24 female.</p>
<section class="rb-block" data-node="step:two"><h3>Sample: Two</h3></section>
<section class="rb-block" data-node="step:emotions"><h3>Coding: Emotions</h3><p class="small">one row for each passage that fits; 29 rows.</p><details><summary>The instruction a second coder would follow</summary><blockquote>Read the interview. Find every passage in which the interviewee (not the interviewer) expresses or reports a feeling of their own: a feeling they have now or have had, in their own words. Loneliness counts as a feeling. A generalised &#x27;you&#x27; or &#x27;it can feel&#x27; counts only where the interviewee ties it in that passage to their own experience. Do not code: feelings the interviewee imagines for a figure in a drawing or for people in general; purely hypothetical feelings (&#x27;I would feel&#x27;); feelings the interviewer puts forward that the interviewee only agrees to with &#x27;yeah&#x27; or &#x27;uhum&#x27;. Where the same feeling about the same thing is repeated or elaborated in follow-up answers, code it once, quoting the clearest words. Where one answer names feelings about two different things, code each. Where one answer names several feelings about the same thing, code them as one passage and name them all in the emotion label; where a later answer names a different feeling about the same thing, code that too. For each passage, copy the words exactly and fill the columns.</blockquote></details><h4>Valence</h4><p>Whether the feeling expressed is pleasant, unpleasant or both.</p><div class="scroll"><table class="codebook"><thead><tr><th>Code</th><th>Means</th><th>Counts</th><th>Does not count</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><th>Positive</th><td>A pleasant feeling: happiness, enjoyment, relief, gratitude, love, hope, ease, respect, calm acceptance.</td><td>Statements such as &#x27;I feel good, happy&#x27;, &#x27;I enjoy&#x27;, &#x27;it&#x27;s alright now&#x27;.</td><td>Neutral description of an activity with no feeling named or plainly expressed.</td></tr><tr><th>Negative</th><td>An unpleasant feeling: sadness, loneliness, frustration, resentment, boredom, unease, longing, resignation, distress.</td><td>Statements such as &#x27;it makes me sad&#x27;, &#x27;I feel quite lonely&#x27;, &#x27;I don&#x27;t feel okay&#x27;.</td><td>A passage that pairs an unpleasant feeling with a pleasant or accepting one about the same thing: code mixed.</td></tr><tr><th>Mixed</th><td>Pleasant and unpleasant feelings, or acceptance and hurt, expressed together about the same thing.</td><td>Statements such as &#x27;conflicting emotions&#x27; or &#x27;I&#x27;m used to it now but sometimes it can get to me&#x27;.</td><td>Two feelings about different things in the same answer: code each separately.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>Emotion</h4><p>A short label, one to three words, for the feeling, such as loneliness, frustration, gratitude, relief; where one passage names several feelings, list each.</p><h4>Subject</h4><p>In a few words, what the feeling is about or what brings it on, as the interviewee describes it.</p></section>
<section class="rb-block" data-node="step:by_person"><h3>Counting: Rows by document and valence <span class="small">(by_person)</span></h3><div class="rb-block" data-node="asset:by_person"><details><summary>The table <span class="small">6 cells</span></summary><div class="scroll"><table class="codebook"><thead><tr><th>Document</th><th>Valence</th><th>Rows</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>I48</td><td>Negative</td><td>11 of None</td></tr><tr><td>I48</td><td>Positive</td><td>7 of None</td></tr><tr><td>I01</td><td>Negative</td><td>6 of None</td></tr><tr><td>I01</td><td>Positive</td><td>3 of None</td></tr><tr><td>I01</td><td>Mixed</td><td>1 of None</td></tr><tr><td>I48</td><td>Mixed</td><td>1 of None</td></tr></tbody></table></div></details></div></section>
<section class="rb-block" data-node="step:answer"><h3>Writing the answer</h3><details><summary>The instruction the answer was written to</summary><blockquote>List the examples of each person&#x27;s positive, negative and mixed feelings, with the counts by person and valence, and set the two people side by side.</blockquote></details></section>
<h3>The checks code ran</h3><ul><li>29 of 29 quotations found word for word in their document.</li><li>6 numbers in the answer, every one counted by code; 0 written by hand.</li><li>0 citations to passages that do not exist; 0 quotations not in the passage they cite.</li></ul>
<section class="rb-block" data-node="step:second_coding"><h3>Second coding</h3><p>A second coder coded the documents afresh to the same definitions, without seeing the first coding.</p></section>
<section class="rb-block" data-node="step:check"><h3>The check</h3><p>A fresh reader ruled on every place the two codings differed, and checked every sentence and quotation of the answer against the documents, correcting the coding and the answer where they were wrong. The answer above is the corrected one, recounted after the check.</p><p class="small">- Disagreements at the level of document and value (recode/disagreements.md): 0.
- Passage-level differences (work/passage_compare_output.md): 11 listed, of which 4 are real disagreements and 7 are not (the same feeling quoted from different words, or unrelated passages paired by word overlap).
- Real disagreements ruled for the rows: 3. Ruled for the second coder: 1. Unsettled by the definition: 0.
- Definition tightened: 1 (in workflow.json, from the second coder&#x27;s notes in recode/unclear.md), with both documents recoded against it.
- Rows changed: 5 (2 removed, 3 edited). I01 positive goes from 4 to 3; I48 negative goes from 12 to 11.
- Sentences changed in answer.md: 15.</p></section>
<h3>The record</h3><p>Code drew this report from the run's zip, in-interviews-i01-person-24-newham-and-i48-woman-23-2026-10-09-revision-2-0e0a0751.zip, with Rubicon 0.1.0+bfb9b34fd622. The zip's fingerprint is <code class="fingerprint">0e0a07515b7a48d795866fb3effd1743af2e83e3af1814bfeb2a7cef932841db</code>. It is the SHA-256 of the zip's SHA256SUMS file, which lists the SHA-256 of every other file in the zip, so changing any file breaks the match. To check that the zip is the one this report was drawn from, unzip it, run <code>sha256sum -c SHA256SUMS</code>, then <code>sha256sum SHA256SUMS</code>, and compare the result with the fingerprint above. Rubicon's own code, which is public, checks the rest: <code>python verify.py &lt;report&gt; &lt;zip&gt;</code> recounts every number from the coded passages in the zip, draws the report again from the zip and says whether it matches this one exactly.</p>
<h3>Revisions</h3><p>Changes made after the first report, each recounted and checked before this version was drawn. Earlier versions keep their own files.</p><ul><li>8 October 2026: asked to refer to the interviewee of I01 as a person, using they/them. Presentation only: wording changed in the answer, in the question as agreed, in four row labels (subject column, free text) and in the audit notes; no definition, value, quotation or row changed, row ids unchanged; recounted with the answer, all counts as before.</li><li>9 October 2026: asked to change the title to &quot;The feelings expressed in two loneliness interviews&quot;. Presentation only: the answer&#x27;s heading changed; no definition, value, quotation, row or count changed; recounted with the answer, all counts as before.</li></ul><h2>Passages cited</h2><p class="note">I01: "I’ve always had people around me but I’ve been more misunderstood, which has led to being lonely."</p><p class="note">I01: "Uuum, quite frustrating. It’s frustrating to be misunderstood."</p><p class="note">I01: "probably, a couple of times in my life where I felt unsupported"</p><p class="note">I01: "I was always just as alone before any of my breakups occurred but when you break up you feel more lonelier after than you was before"</p><p class="note">I01: "I think I feel quite lonely in that moment"</p><p class="note">I01: "I feel completely resentment when I walk through those environments and see those people sleeping rough"</p><p class="note">I01: "I wouldn’t want to contribute to someone’s drug habit, so there’s conflicting emotions"</p><p class="note">I01: "it feels good to go, um I mean I enjoy the barber shop, not just for getting my haircut but for… the experience I get to have by going there and seeing the same people and knowing that they’re doing well and, everyone’s getting by fine, I think…and….I’m grateful to see people that I know and have known for a long time fine."</p><p class="note">I01: "when I choose to kind of lock myself away and do my activities that I choose to do on my own I think I’m quite at ease with that situation"</p><p class="note">I01: "I’ve seen how some people do that the right things with soup kitchens and stuff and I respect and appreciate"</p><p class="note">I48: "it really makes me sad, it makes me feel lonely"</p><p class="note">I48: "Just, I wished I had my mom."</p><p class="note">I48: "I feel like I can’t change that. I can’t bring my mom back."</p><p class="note">I48: "It’s annoying, sad, lonely. [pause]. It makes me wish I can do what they do with their siblings."</p><p class="note">I48: "It’s not a nice feeling, knowing that there’s no one."</p><p class="note">I48: "sometimes I just remember things and I’m not happy about that."</p><p class="note">I48: "I don’t feel okay. I just want to be with somebody."</p><p class="note">I48: "I feel like I need help sometimes"</p><p class="note">I48: "at night before going to bed I sometimes I have to cry myself to sleep because I’m lonely"</p><p class="note">I48: "then I realise, oh my God, there’s no one here. There’s yea…. realise I’m lonely, starts all over again."</p><p class="note">I48: "Just bored cause there’s nothing to do."</p><p class="note">I48: "I feel like I’m used to it now but sometimes it can get to me."</p><p class="note">I48: "It makes worry-free around my friends."</p><p class="note">I48: "I feel good, happy."</p><p class="note">I48: "I see people having fun, then I can have fun myself. It makes me forget about, you know, life"</p><p class="note">I48: "Good, relieved."</p><p class="note">I48: "I love kids so much"</p><p class="note">I48: "I just hope that one day I’ll have my own child to show that kind of love."</p><p class="note">I48: "It’s alright now. It’s cool."</p><h2>What next</h2><p><b>For the evaluator:</b> Treat this report as a draft. Before it is final, make sense of it with the people it concerns, such as programme staff and participants: whether the findings ring true, what they leave out, and what to do about them. You can then use the Rubicon plugin to work their responses back into a revised answer.</p><p><b>For evaluation commissioners:</b> Parallel to this report is a zip file (in-interviews-i01-person-24-newham-and-i48-woman-23-2026-10-09-revision-2-0e0a0751.zip), saved in the same folder, which contains the whole run: the documents, the coded passages and the workflow that recounts every number. Its fingerprint, under How this was made, ties it to this report: change a file in either and they no longer match. With it, anyone can check every number against the coded passages, and code the documents again to the same definitions without Rubicon or any other particular software. A fresh coding will not reproduce this report word for word, because AI models are unpredictable, but it should come close.</p><p>The Rubicon plugin for Claude is provided for free by Causal Map Ltd. Causal Map also runs workshops and consultancy on analysing qualitative evidence for evaluation: <a href="https://causalmap.app/contact/?utm_source=rubicon-plugin&amp;utm_medium=report">get in touch</a>, or follow <a href="https://www.linkedin.com/company/causalmap/">Causal Map on LinkedIn</a>. Coming soon: continue your Rubicon work at the Rubicon website.</p></body></html>